.TH OVERVIEW 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
overview \- report a Mach\-O file's whole structural picture in one call
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B overview
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-symbols ]
.RB [ \-\-strings ]
.RB [ \-\-max=\fIn\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-min=\fIn\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-compact ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reports every slice in a Mach\-O file with its segments, sections, load commands,
header flags, UUID and entry point, and on request the symbol table and the
contents of its C\-string sections.
.PP
Answering this completely with the other tools costs four invocations, and each
of them chooses which slice of a universal binary to read independently. This
reads the file once, so a symbol table and a list of strings in the same answer
always come from the same architecture, and the result names which one that was.
.PP
The structure is exactly what
.BR describe (1)
reports, field for field, so
.I .slices[0].sections
means the same thing in both. This tool adds to that answer and never replaces
part of it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-symbols
Include the symbol table: defined names, deduplicated by name and sorted by
address. Imported names are counted but not listed, since they all carry address
zero and would bury the real ones.
.TP
.B \-\-strings
Include the contents of the C\-string sections.
.TP
.BI \-\-max= n
Cap on each inventory. The default is 4000; 0 means unlimited. Whatever the cap
drops is reported as
.B truncated
rather than silently shortened.
.TP
.BI \-\-min= n
Shortest string to report. The default is 4.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
Write the JSON on one line instead of indenting it. Measured at 41% smaller on
an inventory of 4,000 rows. Has no effect on the text output.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send prose to standard error.
Addresses are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings rather than numbers, because a 64\-bit virtual address does not survive
a JSON number.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SH "WHAT IS NOT READ"
Every result carries a
.B notRead
list, and the text output prints it on every run. This package names load
commands but does not interpret them, and it does not read the code signature,
entitlements, the export trie, chained fixups, Objective\-C or Swift metadata,
dSYM or DWARF, FAT32 containers, or the dylib an
.B LC_LOAD_DYLIB
names.
.PP
The list is in the output on purpose. A JSON object that looks exhaustive and is
silent about what it skipped cannot be told apart from one that genuinely has
nothing to report, and that difference is the difference between a fact and a
gap. See
.BR README.md
section \(lqWhat it will not do\(rq for why these are excluded.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given on the command line.
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was read.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read, or is not a Mach\-O.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
overview /usr/local/go/bin/go
overview \-\-symbols /usr/local/go/bin/go
overview \-\-json \-\-compact \-\-symbols /usr/local/go/bin/go | jq '.data.symbols.count'
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
An empty inventory and an unimplemented one are different answers and are
reported differently. A binary with no
.B __cstring
section yields zero strings with a note saying so, which is a fact about the
file; a stripped slice yields no defined symbols with a note saying that, which
is a fact about the slice. Neither is reported as an empty list on its own.
.PP
There is deliberately no equivalent tool on the MCP server, which makes the same
argument about the same bundling: see
.BR mcp (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR describe (1),
.BR sym (1),
.BR findliteral (1),
.BR audit (1),
.BR fingerprint (1),
.BR diff (1),
.BR otool (1),
.BR lipo (1)
